From ca774f36f2c875dfc890a89a0a60923796341b70 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JohnnyC96 <54557697+JohnnyC96@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 18:25:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 97/97] Runtime: NETCNFIF's settings-file server, the PS2
 network settings made for the player

A game using Sony's NETCNFIF reads its network settings (netcnf's BWNETCNF set) through file callbacks that call
the EE RPC server 0x80001101 (ps2sdk netcnfif.c: 12 open, 13 read, 14 close), which the game registers to read a
memory card. With PS2X_NETCNF set ("dhcp", or "static:address,netmask,gateway,dns1[,dns2]"), the host adapter
serves that id itself (IopHost::hasEeRpcServer/eeRpc, patch 0096) with the four files generated in
runtime/ps2_netcnf: the index "BWNETCNF" and "net000.cnf" in plain text, "ifc000.dat" and "dev000.dat" scrambled
as netcnf does (three rotation amounts per console-ID byte, each 16-bit word rotated left then inverted; console
ID = patch 0093's eight 0xAA bytes). The player needs neither a memory card nor Sony's configuration utility.
Unset or "off": as before.

Tests (written first): ps2x_tests Netcnf: the scrambling of a known word for two IDs and an odd last byte; the
DHCP set byte-for-byte against the reference writer (outbreak-modern tools/ob/mcd.py, checked against the game's
netcnf.irx); a static set's keywords; PS2X_NETCNF parsing; RPC 12/13/14 open, read (clipped), close, closed and
unknown names. Fails without: 558/569.
